    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Declined to equip Lavaspur Boots or Shadowspear to Pinnacle Emissary before combat, despite the opponent being completely tapped out. Equipping Shadowspear (2 mana) would give +1/+1, trample, and lifelink, allowing Pinnacle Emissary to attack as a 4/4 trampler with lifelink past Formidable Speaker (2/4). Even Lavaspur Boots (1 mana) would add +1/+0 and ward {1} for protection. They still had plenty of mana/artifacts to hold up Metallic Rebuke via improvise.",
      "action_taken": "Passed on all equipment activations in precombat main phase",
      "better_line": "Equip Shadowspear to Pinnacle Emissary for 2 mana, making it a 4/4 with trample and lifelink, then attack. Still can hold up Metallic Rebuke with remaining mana and improvise.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 87,
      "player": "Kimi25 Analyst",
      "decision_index": 158,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The opponent is at 12 life with all lands tapped and only Formidable Speaker (2/4, no flying) and Overlord of the Balemurk (5/5) as potential blockers. Pinnacle Emissary is a 4/3 (likely pumped by Shadowspear or counters from artifact ETBs). The Drone Token has flying so it can't be blocked by ground creatures. If both attack, Overlord could block the Emissary (trading 4/3 into 5/5, Emissary dies) or Formidable Speaker could block (2/4 blocks 4/3, Speaker dies). But the opponent's lands are all tapped, meaning no combat tricks. Attacking with both forces the opponent to make unfavorable blocks - if they block Emissary with the 5/5, the Drone still gets through for 1 (total 1 damage but Emissary is preserved if they don't block it). Actually, if all lands are tapped, the opponent can't activate Formidable Speaker's untap ability either. Attacking with both means: opponent blocks Emissary with Overlord 5/5 (Emissary dies, Overlord lives) and Drone gets 1 through, or blocks Emissary with Speaker 2/4 (Speaker dies, Emissary lives) and Drone gets 1 through, or doesn't block at all (5 damage total). The best case is 5 damage (opponent to 7), worst case is losing Emissary but dealing 1. Given the opponent has all lands tapped, attacking with both is better - the Emissary likely survives since opponent probably blocks with Speaker to save Overlord, and you deal 5 damage instead of 1. Only attacking with the Drone for 1 damage is overly conservative and misses 4 potential damage.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked with only the 1/1 Drone Token for 1 damage.",
      "better_line": "Attack with both Pinnacle Emissary and Drone Token. With all opponent lands tapped, opponent must make unfavorable blocking decisions. If unblocked, deal 5 damage (opponent to 7). If Emissary is blocked by Speaker 2/4, Speaker dies and you still deal 1 with Drone. This is significantly more pressure.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 88,
      "player": "Kimi25 Analyst",
      "decision_index": 163,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Kimi25 chose to attack with only Pinnacle Emissary (a 4/3 with the Drone Token also available as a 1/1 flyer). The opponent is at 12 life with all lands tapped and only Formidable Speaker (2/4) and Overlord of the Balemurk (5/5) as potential blockers. The Drone Token has flying and likely cannot be blocked by either ground creature (Overlord is 5/5 without flying, Formidable Speaker is 2/4 without flying). Attacking with both Pinnacle Emissary and the Drone Token would deal an additional 1 damage for free. There's essentially no downside to sending the Drone Token as well since it has flying and the opponent has no flyers on board.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked with only Pinnacle Emissary (4/3), leaving the Drone Token back.",
      "better_line": "Attack with both Pinnacle Emissary and the Drone Token to deal 1 extra unblockable flying damage, putting the opponent to 11 (or lower depending on blocks).",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 88,
      "player": "Kimi25 Analyst",
      "decision_index": 163,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Metallic Rebuke was used to counter Shardless Agent, but in standard Magic rules, cascade triggers on cast and resolves before the original spell. By the time priority is available to counter the Agent, the cascade trigger has likely already resolved, meaning the counter only stops the 2/2 body. Using Metallic Rebuke on a 2/2 body is a poor use of the counterspell, especially when holding Kappa Cannoneer that needs protection from removal. The player's reasoning incorrectly assumes countering stops cascade.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Metallic Rebuke to counter Shardless Agent, believing it would prevent cascade",
      "better_line": "Let Shardless Agent resolve (the 2/2 is not a significant threat) and save Metallic Rebuke to protect Kappa Cannoneer when deploying it, or to counter a more impactful spell from the opponent",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 95,
      "player": "Kimi25 Analyst",
      "decision_index": 179,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "By stacking Overlord of the Balemurk to resolve last (after Formidable Speaker), they miss the opportunity to mill 4 cards first, which could provide better information and a returned creature card before deciding what to discard to Formidable Speaker's tutor ability. Resolving Overlord first would let them see what gets milled, return a creature to hand, and then have more discard options for the tutor.",
      "action_taken": "Ordered triggers so Formidable Speaker resolves first (discard Endurance to tutor), then Generous Ent, then Overlord of the Balemurk last.",
      "better_line": "Order triggers so Overlord of the Balemurk resolves first (mill 4, return a creature), then Formidable Speaker (discard with more information and options), then Generous Ent.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 97,
      "player": "Gem3F Villain",
      "decision_index": 183,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Engineered Explosives has 0 charge counters, so activating it destroys all nonland permanents with MV 0. This destroys their own Mox Opal while hitting nothing on the opponent's side. This wastes two artifacts (EE sacrificed as cost + Mox Opal destroyed) for no benefit, reducing their artifact count for affinity/improvise/metalcraft.",
      "action_taken": "Activated Engineered Explosives (0 counters) to destroy all MV-0 nonland permanents, destroying their own Mox Opal for no gain.",
      "better_line": "Cast Emry for {U} (affinity reducing cost with 4 artifacts), then use remaining mana and artifacts to cast Kappa Cannoneer via improvise, developing the board with real threats instead of destroying own resources.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 104,
      "player": "Kimi25 Analyst",
      "decision_index": 195,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "At 9 life with 2 untapped lands and Food tokens about to be destroyed by Engineered Explosives (0 counters), Gem3F passed without sacrificing a Food token for 3 life. The Food tokens were destroyed for no benefit when at least one could have been cashed in for 3 life.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority without activating any Food tokens, allowing all three to be destroyed by Engineered Explosives.",
      "better_line": "Sacrifice one Food token for 3 life using the 2 available mana (Underground Mortuary + Breeding Pool), going from 9 to 12 life before the tokens are destroyed anyway.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 105,
      "player": "Gem3F Villain",
      "decision_index": 197,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Gem3F Villain had Endurance (flash) in hand and could have cast it targeting Kimi25 Analyst before Living End resolved. This would have put all of Kimi25's graveyard (including Pinnacle Emissary, Arcbound Ravager, Aether Spellbomb, etc.) on the bottom of their library, so when Living End resolves, the opponent gets back zero creatures while Gem3F gets back all their graveyard creatures. Instead, by passing, Living End will return creatures to both sides.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority without casting Endurance, allowing Living End to resolve with the opponent's graveyard intact.",
      "better_line": "Cast Endurance targeting Kimi25 Analyst to empty their graveyard before Living End resolves, ensuring a massively one-sided Living End that only returns Gem3F's creatures.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 113,
      "player": "Gem3F Villain",
      "decision_index": 220,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Villain declined to cast Endurance (flash, 3/4 reach) while the opponent has Emry on the battlefield with a graveyard full of powerful artifacts (Mox Opal, Arcbound Ravager, Engineered Explosives, Aether Spellbomb, etc.). Shuffling away that graveyard would significantly weaken Emry's value and deny the opponent access to key recursive threats. Additionally, Endurance provides a solid body on a board where Villain is at 9 life.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority without casting Endurance, keeping it in hand.",
      "better_line": "Cast Endurance targeting the opponent to shuffle away their artifact-rich graveyard, neutering Emry's recursion engine while adding a 3/4 reach blocker to the board.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 115,
      "player": "Gem3F Villain",
      "decision_index": 224,
    }
  ],
